---
title: Potato latkes
servings: 6
yield: 18 latkes
tags: [jewish, vegetarian, hanukkah, holiday, winter]
cuisine: jewish
time: 1 hour
source: Crispy Latkes by Jennifer Segal, Once Upon a Chef, https://www.onceuponachef.com/recipes/latkes.html
---

Peel the @russet potatoes{2%lb}(2 to 3 potatoes). Coarsely grate them with the @yellow onion{1}(medium, peeled) on a #box grater{} or the grating disc of a food processor.

Tip the mixture into a #fine sieve{} and press out as much liquid as you can with paper towels or a clean cloth. Repeat with dry towels until it is mostly drained.

Beat the @eggs{2}(large) in a #large bowl{}. Add the potato and onion, @salt{1.5%tsp}, @baking powder{2%tsp} and @all-purpose flour{1/4%cup} and mix well.

Heat about 1/4 cup of @vegetable oil{}(for frying) in a #large skillet{}, cast iron or nonstick, over medium-high heat until it shimmers.

Drop in mounds of about 3 tbsp of batter. They should sizzle. Flatten each one a little with a spatula.

Turn the heat to medium and cook ~{5%minutes} until the bottoms are golden and crisp. Flip and cook ~{4-5%minutes} more.

Drain on a paper-towel-lined baking sheet. Fry the rest, adding oil as needed.

Serve warm with @sour cream{} and @applesauce{}.

> Squeeze the potatoes hard. Wet shreds steam instead of crisping.
